      "notes": "All claims are well-grounded in their cited evidence. The tier assignments are appropriate: E15 and E16 describe enacted/proposed legislative provisions and are reasonably classed as measurable (what the bill contains), not projections. E22 is correctly classed projected. No tier_integrity issues. The direction 'worsens' with moderate magnitude is well-supported: mass monitoring without individual suspicion (E15), direct bank deductions and driving licence disqualification (E16) are clear O10 expansions of coercive state reach. The rationale correctly scopes the UC rollout as liberty-neutral and focuses on the Fraud Bill. No party-blind issues; reasoning is mechanism-based. No false_balance issue \u2014 direction is 'worsens', not mixed, and the claims support that. One minor note: E15 is sourced partly from walesonline.co.uk and youtube.com alongside parliamentary sources; the parliamentary/government sources suffice to anchor the claim. No lever_leakage detected \u2014 the verdict scores the liberty effect, not the anti-fraud policy goal itself."
